 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410848 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410854 1079 #uuid "ca3e15c9-c3c6-56d5-be44-2ff142682b75" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410851 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410855 1069 "q-39157" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410855 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -10 \\\\ 17 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410855 1072 #uuid "59a82201-03c5-57db-ad1f-7c04fbb98da0" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410855 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3-\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -10 \\\\ 17 & -10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13+\\text{i} & -10 \\\\ 17 & -13+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 17{x}_{1}+(-13+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=10$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 13+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{10}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{13}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{17}.\n$$" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410853 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410856 1069 "q-99416" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410856 1071 "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410856 1072 #uuid "b89eb3d0-aaac-5e32-8106-610f887f220d" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410856 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-1-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410852 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410857 1069 "q-99345" 13194139534121 17592186045417 true] [17592186410857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 1319413953412